(To determine the laws which regulate this distribution, is the principal) Tj T*
(problem in Political Economy: much as the science has been improved by) Tj T*
(the writings of Turgot, Stuart, Smith, Say, Sismondi, and others, they) Tj T*
(afford very little satisfactory information respecting the natural) Tj T*
(course of rent, profit, and wages.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In 1815, Mr. Malthus in his "Inquiry into the Nature and Progress of) Tj T*
(Rent," and a Fellow of University College, Oxford, in his "Essay on the) Tj T*
(Application of Capital to Land," presented to the world, nearly at the) Tj T*
(same moment, the true doctrine of rent; without a knowledge of which it) Tj T*
(is impossible to understand the effect of the progress of wealth on) Tj T*
(profits and wages, or to trace satisfactorily the influence of taxation) Tj T*
(on different classes of the community, particularly when the commodities) Tj T*
(taxed are the productions immediately derived from the surface of the) Tj T*
(earth. Adam Smith, and the other able writers to whom I have alluded,) Tj T*
(not having viewed correctly the principles of rent, have, it appears to) Tj T*
(me, overlooked many important truths, which can only be discovered after) Tj T*
(the subject of rent is thoroughly understood.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To supply this deficiency, abilities are required of a far superior cast) Tj T*
(to any possessed by the writer of the following pages; yet after having) Tj T*
(given to this subject his best consideration--after the aid which he has) Tj T*
(derived from the works of the above-mentioned eminent writers--and after) Tj T*
(the valuable experience which a few late years, abounding in facts, have) Tj T*
(yielded to the present generation--it will not, he trusts, be deemed) Tj T*
(presumptuous in him to state his opinions on the laws of profits and) Tj T*
(wages, and on the operation of taxes. If the principles which he deems) Tj T*
(correct should be found to be so, it will be for others more able than) Tj T*
(himself to trace them to all their important consequences.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The writer, in combating received opinions, has found it necessary to) Tj T*
(advert more particularly to those passages in the writings of Adam Smith) Tj T*
(from which he sees reason to differ; but he hopes it will not on that) Tj T*
(account be suspected that he does not, in common with all those who) Tj T*
(acknowledge the importance of the science of Political Economy,) Tj T*
(participate in the admiration which the profound work of this) Tj T*
(celebrated author so justly excites.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The same remark may be applied to the excellent works of M. Say, who not) Tj T*
(only was the first, or among the first, of continental writers, who) Tj T*
(justly appreciated and applied the principles of Smith, and who has done) Tj T*
(more than all other continental writers taken together, to recommend the) Tj T*
(principles of that enlightened and beneficial system to the nations of) Tj T*
(Europe; but who has succeeded in placing the science in a more logical,) Tj T*
(and more instructive order; and has enriched it by several discussions,) Tj T*
(original, accurate, and profound.[1] The respect, however, which the) Tj T*
(author entertains for the writings of this gentleman, has not prevented) Tj T*
(him from commenting with that freedom which he thinks the interests of) Tj T*
(science require, on such passages of the "Economie Politique," as) Tj T*
(appeared at variance with his own ideas.) Tj T*
