(CHAPTER I.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(ON VALUE.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It has been observed by Adam Smith, that "the word Value has two) Tj T*
(different meanings, and sometimes expresses the utility of some) Tj T*
(particular object, and sometimes the power of purchasing other goods) Tj T*
(which the possession of that object conveys. The one may be called) Tj T*
(_value in use_; the other, _value in exchange_. The things," he) Tj T*
(continues, "which have the greatest value in use, have frequently little) Tj T*
(or no value in exchange; and, on the contrary, those which have the) Tj T*
(greatest value in exchange, have little or no value in use." Water and) Tj T*
(air are abundantly useful; they are indeed indispensable to existence,) Tj T*
(yet, under ordinary circumstances, nothing can be obtained in exchange) Tj T*
(for them. Gold, on the contrary, though of little use compared with air) Tj T*
(or water, will exchange for a great quantity of other goods.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Utility then is not the measure of exchangeable value, although it is) Tj T*
(absolutely essential to it. If a commodity were in no way useful,--in) Tj T*
(other words, if it could in no way contribute to our gratification,--it) Tj T*
(would be destitute of exchangeable value, however scarce it might be, or) Tj T*
(whatever quantity of labour might be necessary to procure it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Possessing utility, commodities derive their exchangeable value from two) Tj T*
(sources: from their scarcity, and from the quantity of labour required) Tj T*
(to obtain them.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There are some commodities, the value of which is determined by their) Tj T*
(scarcity alone. No labour can increase the quantity of such goods, and) Tj T*
