(therefore their value cannot be lowered by an increased supply. Some) Tj T*
(rare statues and pictures, scarce books and coins, wines of a peculiar) Tj T*
(quality, which can be made only from grapes grown on a particular soil,) Tj T*
(of which there is a very limited quantity, are all of this description.) Tj T*
(Their value is wholly independent of the quantity of labour originally) Tj T*
(necessary to produce them, and varies with the varying wealth and) Tj T*
(inclinations of those who are desirous to possess them.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(These commodities, however, form a very small part of the mass of) Tj T*
(commodities daily exchanged in the market. By far the greatest part of) Tj T*
(those goods which are the objects of desire, are procured by labour; and) Tj T*
(they may be multiplied, not in one country alone, but in many, almost) Tj T*
(without any assignable limit, if we are disposed to bestow the labour) Tj T*
(necessary to obtain them.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In speaking then of commodities, of their exchangeable value, and of the) Tj T*
(laws which regulate their relative prices, we mean always such) Tj T*
(commodities only as can be increased in quantity by the exertion of) Tj T*
(human industry, and on the production of which competition operates) Tj T*
(without restraint.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the early stages of society, the exchangeable value of these) Tj T*
(commodities, or the rule which determines how much of one shall be) Tj T*
(given in exchange for another, depends solely on the comparative) Tj T*
(quantity of labour expended on each.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("The real price of every thing," says Adam Smith, "what every thing) Tj T*
(really costs to the man who wants to acquire it, is the toil and trouble) Tj T*
(of acquiring it. What every thing is really worth to the man who has) Tj T*
(acquired it, and who wants to dispose of it, or exchange it for) Tj T*
(something else, is the toil and trouble which it can save to himself,) Tj T*
(and which it can impose upon other people." "Labour was the first) Tj T*
(price--the original purchase-money that was paid for all things." Again,) Tj T*
("in that early and rude state of society, which precedes both the) Tj T*
(accumulation of stock and the appropriation of land, the proportion) Tj T*
(between the quantities of labour necessary for acquiring different) Tj T*
(objects, seems to be the only circumstance which can afford any rule for) Tj T*
(exchanging them for one another. If among a nation of hunters, for) Tj T*
(example, it usually cost twice the labour to kill a beaver which it does) Tj T*
(to kill a deer, one beaver should naturally exchange for, or be worth) Tj T*
(two deer. It is natural that what is usually the produce of two days',) Tj T*
(or two hours' labour, should be worth double of what is usually the) Tj T*
(produce of one day's, or one hour's labour."[2]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(That this is really the foundation of the exchangeable value of all) Tj T*
(things, excepting those which cannot be increased by human industry, is) Tj T*
(a doctrine of the utmost importance in political economy; for from no) Tj T*
(source do so many errors, and so much difference of opinion in that) Tj T*
(science proceed, as from the vague ideas, which are attached to the word) Tj T*
(value.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
