(exchangeable value, every increase of the quantity of labour must) Tj T*
(augment the value of that commodity on which it is exercised, as every) Tj T*
(diminution must lower it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Adam Smith, who so accurately defined the original source of) Tj T*
(exchangeable value, and who was bound in consistency to maintain, that) Tj T*
(all things became more or less valuable in proportion as more or less) Tj T*
(labour was bestowed on their production, has himself erected another) Tj T*
(standard measure of value, and speaks of things being more or less) Tj T*
(valuable, in proportion as they will exchange for more or less of this) Tj T*
(standard measure. Sometimes he speaks of corn, at other times of labour,) Tj T*
(as a standard measure; not the quantity of labour bestowed on the) Tj T*
(production of any object, but the quantity which it can command in the) Tj T*
(market: as if these were two equivalent expressions, and as if because a) Tj T*
(man's labour had become doubly efficient, and he could therefore produce) Tj T*
(twice the quantity of a commodity, he would necessarily receive twice) Tj T*
(the former quantity in exchange for it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If this indeed were true, if the reward of the labourer were always in) Tj T*
(proportion to what he produced, the quantity of labour bestowed on a) Tj T*
(commodity, and the quantity of labour which that commodity would) Tj T*
(purchase, would be equal, and either might accurately measure the) Tj T*
(variations of other things: but they are not equal; the first is under) Tj T*
(many circumstances an invariable standard, indicating correctly the) Tj T*
(variations of other things; the latter is subject to as many) Tj T*
(fluctuations as the commodities compared with it. Adam Smith, after most) Tj T*
(ably shewing the insufficiency of a variable medium, such as gold and) Tj T*
(silver, for the purpose of determining the varying value of other) Tj T*
(things, has himself, by fixing on corn or labour, chosen a medium no) Tj T*
(less variable.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Gold and silver are no doubt subject to fluctuations, from the discovery) Tj T*
(of new and more abundant mines; but such discoveries are rare, and their) Tj T*
(effects, though powerful, are limited to periods of comparatively short) Tj T*
(duration. They are subject also to fluctuation, from improvements in the) Tj T*
(skill and machinery with which the mines may be worked; as in) Tj T*
(consequence of such improvements, a greater quantity may be obtained) Tj T*
(with the same labour. They are further subject to fluctuation from the) Tj T*
(decreasing produce of the mines, after they have yielded a supply to the) Tj T*
(world, for a succession of ages. But from which of these sources of) Tj T*
(fluctuation is corn exempted? Does not that also vary, on one hand, from) Tj T*
(improvements in agriculture, from improved machinery and implements) Tj T*
(used in husbandry, as well as from the discovery of new tracts of) Tj T*
(fertile land, which in other countries may be taken into cultivation,) Tj T*
(and which will affect the value of corn in every market where) Tj T*
(importation is free? Is it not on the other hand subject to be enhanced) Tj T*
(in value from prohibitions of importation, from increasing population) Tj T*
(and wealth, and the greater difficulty of obtaining the increased) Tj T*
(supplies, on account of the additional quantity of labour which the) Tj T*
(cultivation of inferior lands requires? Is not the value of labour) Tj T*
(equally variable; being not only affected, as all other things are, by) Tj T*
(the proportion between the supply and demand, which uniformly varies) Tj T*
